## Hiring a GEO specialist vs a GEO agency

A GEO specialist, typically a sole consultant or a two-to-three-person team, works directly on your account, understands your entity and content set in detail, and usually costs less than a full agency retainer. The trade-off is capacity: a specialist cannot run a large content programme or a big digital PR campaign alongside GEO work. Sunny Patel SEO on this list is a specialist example, and says so plainly in its own considerations.

A GEO agency, from a boutique like Semetrical or Screaming Frog through to a full-service shop like Rise at Seven or Impression Digital, brings more people and more channels: content teams, digital PR, technical SEO, and in some cases paid media, all under one contract. That suits a brand that wants generative-engine work coordinated alongside a bigger organic or paid programme, and can absorb the higher retainer that comes with it.

Neither model is inherently better. Ask any provider, specialist or agency, for a documented AI citation before signing; capacity and price differ, but the evidence bar for genuine GEO capability should not.

---

## How to Choose a GEO Agency

Most providers advertising "GEO" cannot demonstrate a single citation. These markers separate genuine generative-engine capability from a repositioned services page:

1. **A documented citation or conversion.** A screenshot of the brand named in an AI Overview, ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Copilot, ideally with a lead attached.
2. **Entity authority capability.** Understanding of how LLMs identify and trust entities. See [what is entity SEO](/blog/what-is-entity-seo/).
3. **Structured-data fluency.** The markup that helps AI crawlers extract a page accurately.
4. **A measurement method.** How they track citations and generative-engine visibility over time, not just rankings.
5. **Published methodology, not slogans.** Evidence of *how* they work, weighted above testimonials.

If your brand is missing from generative answers, start with [why your brand is not appearing in ChatGPT](/blog/brand-not-appearing-in-chatgpt/), then build the foundation with [how to build topical authority](/blog/how-to-build-topical-authority/). The discipline that applies to any SEO hire is set out in [how to choose an SEO consultant](/blog/how-to-choose-seo-consultant/).

---

## Questions to Ask Any GEO Provider

1. **"Show me a brand you have made appear in a generative answer."** A screenshot from an AI Overview, ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Copilot. No example means no proof.
2. **"How do you build and measure entity authority?"** A real answer references corroboration, structured data, and citation tracking, not just "we optimise for AI".
3. **"Is GEO a separate add-on or built into the strategy?"** Genuine GEO comes from the same content and entity foundations as good SEO; a premium standalone "AI" line item with no results is a red flag.
4. **"What changed in your method over the last six months?"** Generative search moves fast. A provider who cannot answer is not keeping up.
5. **"What do I own if we stop after three months?"** You should keep all strategy, audits, and content.

---

## How we ranked these GEO agencies

This list was built from four checks, in order of weight:

1. **A genuine GEO or AI search service page**, naming Generative Engine Optimisation, AI search, or the specific generative engines (AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Copilot) explicitly, rather than an "AI" mention buried in a broader SEO page.
2. **Published case studies or client evidence**, checked directly on the agency's own site, favouring any result tied specifically to an AI Overview, citation, or generative-engine outcome over a generic traffic or ranking number.
3. **My own AI-citation evidence**, where I have it: the Express Medicals conversion via Bing Copilot is the only documented AI-search lead conversion among the providers listed.
4. **Pricing transparency**, noting plainly where a figure is published and where it is not, rather than presenting an estimate as fact.

Every service page and case study cited on this page was checked directly during this update. Several sites claiming "GEO agency" services for individual UK towns, including some appearing prominently in search results for Edinburgh, Exeter, and Leicester, were checked and excluded because they could not be verified as a real local team or office at the time of writing.

